    "segments": [
      {
        "role": "professor lin",
        "speaker": "Professor Lin",
        "sourceStart": 15,
        "sourceEnd": 551,
        "text": "Good morning, everyone. Today we look at unintended policy consequences. Let me start with a small example. A city council wanted to reduce traffic downtown. So they built a new ring road around the city center. The road was wide and fast. Drivers loved it. But within two years, traffic downtown increased by fifteen percent. Why? Because the new road made driving into the city easier. More people decided to drive. This is called induced demand. Although the council built the road to reduce traffic, it actually attracted more cars.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "2d49ed38f7a2173ee80ed74c305dbb134119a286b4efc12649efa803dddd305f",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "student a",
        "speaker": "Student A",
        "sourceStart": 565,
        "sourceEnd": 623,
        "text": "Professor, does that mean we should never build new roads?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "3c003ee5c71745bdc9d1a0b80eb57408fcfedc1f9d33f11efb452f383b873339",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "professor lin",
        "speaker": "Professor Lin",
        "sourceStart": 641,
        "sourceEnd": 817,
        "text": "Not exactly. We need to think about conditions. If a city builds new roads but also improves public transport, then the result may be different. The key is to combine policies.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "e4265dfbd8c7d9cb1b1b8cf033fb5ed3fe664cbab00088b5e846da0a9c81648b",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "student b",
        "speaker": "Student B",
        "sourceStart": 831,
        "sourceEnd": 1197,
        "text": "I read about a similar case with plastic bags. A government banned free plastic bags in supermarkets. Shoppers had to pay for them. At first, plastic bag use dropped by eighty percent. But then people started buying thicker, reusable bags. Those bags were made of more plastic. So total plastic production went up. The policy reduced one problem but created another.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "6cd1e0b6ff20544f4f62b9975e2320b48ff8a0ee0a5fb709d963cffcb58cab74",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "professor lin",
        "speaker": "Professor Lin",
        "sourceStart": 1215,
        "sourceEnd": 1352,
        "text": "Excellent example. This shows a trade-off. When you make one behavior costly, people may switch to another behavior that is also harmful.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "3cf65a7746b3e231df3be1796bf5da3fd32b0f06055c966874c08ed6449f3171",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "student c",
        "speaker": "Student C",
        "sourceStart": 1366,
        "sourceEnd": 1426,
        "text": "What about the tax on sugary drinks? Some cities tried that.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-EricNeural",
        "textHash": "51877de0b76efe979ca00e8ddc8b72e4e9e91d126276728ff23786d91804a996",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "professor lin",
        "speaker": "Professor Lin",
        "sourceStart": 1444,
        "sourceEnd": 1662,
        "text": "Yes. A tax on sugary drinks can reduce consumption. However, if the tax is too high, people may buy cheaper, unhealthy alternatives, like energy drinks with even more sugar. So the effect depends on how people respond.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "dcfb9eb87bdf12e67d22dbae71374fa9cc64181160affa4c1f3eada31a412ae6",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "student a",
        "speaker": "Student A",
        "sourceStart": 1676,
        "sourceEnd": 1713,
        "text": "So policies always have side effects?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "3a925b843c2ff2a2a606d89c9fb42da83a95e558b004bcee893b708dd81568b1",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "professor lin",
        "speaker": "Professor Lin",
        "sourceStart": 1731,
        "sourceEnd": 2153,
        "text": "Almost always. But we can predict some of them. We use models. A model is a simplified picture of reality. It helps us ask: if we do X, what else might happen? For example, a rent control policy sets a maximum rent. It helps current tenants pay less. But if landlords cannot earn enough, they may stop renting out apartments. Then the supply of housing falls. In the long run, rent control can make housing harder to find.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "a9c39b37be3bbb133d39080e21bba21b2b39ea88448116409b042f813ba09734",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "student b",
        "speaker": "Student B",
        "sourceStart": 2167,
        "sourceEnd": 2186,
        "text": "That sounds unfair.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "db2733dd33dceb600be7188e43a34830df3a0db882c7fb0acd1bc07742541dbc",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "professor lin",
        "speaker": "Professor Lin",
        "sourceStart": 2204,
        "sourceEnd": 2356,
        "text": "It is a dilemma. The policy helps some people now but may hurt others later. Good policy design tries to balance short-term help with long-term effects.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "68e52a141def32605a39fe00442845ea7910284d58e582eed8311b1849978ef4",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "student c",
        "speaker": "Student C",
        "sourceStart": 2370,
        "sourceEnd": 2404,
        "text": "How do we know if a policy worked?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-EricNeural",
        "textHash": "1ee9fa28c2816a7fbb6e1ac2950dc5045cd22ba331ca5f076c8b410fef7ffebd",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "professor lin",
        "speaker": "Professor Lin",
        "sourceStart": 2422,
        "sourceEnd": 2626,
        "text": "We compare what happened with what would have happened without the policy. That is called a counterfactual. It is hard because we cannot see the alternative world. But we can use data from similar cities.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "32b35b6f12560ca90d9adb2ceba28b645c7fe3807f9069c27307caf77d6829a8",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "student a",
        "speaker": "Student A",
        "sourceStart": 2640,
        "sourceEnd": 2695,
        "text": "So the main lesson is to think carefully before we act?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "5c08af02e43387d7061a7153a390d6458575b1a0b76eae0d59439123f993de34",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "professor lin",
        "speaker": "Professor Lin",
        "sourceStart": 2713,
        "sourceEnd": 2813,
        "text": "Yes. And to monitor results after we act. Policies are experiments. We must be ready to adjust them.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "374dbcbba59ae995a14c882927a78fbeb6b2ec7b351afb1532cffd596c9330fb",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "student b",
        "speaker": "Student B",
        "sourceStart": 2827,
        "sourceEnd": 2869,
        "text": "Thank you, Professor. This was very clear.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "a53987845c4b5eb9d7b46d44194420d008778edf24d9e645a4d5d2d910ced740",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "professor lin",
        "speaker": "Professor Lin",
        "sourceStart": 2887,
        "sourceEnd": 2977,
        "text": "You're welcome. Next week we will discuss how to communicate policy changes to the public.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "fdfa808cf866f3923f48fb6c82f8497c768b9db70363068d32c759795d095c8e",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      }
    ]
  },
